    "topicId": "addiction",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Addiction makes large promises and then takes more than it gives. It may have become a refuge, reward, anesthetic, or master, but it does not own the final word over you. Jesus receives people who come into the light without bargaining or concealment. His mercy does not minimize the wreckage; it gives you a place to confess it honestly, receive forgiveness, and begin a life in which your body, desires, money, time, and relationships are increasingly offered to God rather than ruled by a substance or behavior.\n\nDo not wait to feel strong before becoming truthful. Bring the whole pattern into the light with mature believers, accept medical help where withdrawal or bodily danger is possible, and welcome boundaries that interrupt access and secrecy. A stumble is serious, but it is not permission to surrender; tell the truth quickly and return to the path. Measure progress not only by abstinence but also by growing honesty, worship, responsibility, restitution, patience, and love. You will need more than private resolve. Let the church help you build a life of prayer, work, rest, service, and accountable friendship in which Christ becomes more precious and obedience becomes practiced rather than merely intended.",
