    "definition": "Anger is a morally meaningful response to something judged wrong; it may reflect righteous concern, selfish desire, wounded pride, fear, grief, or several motives at once. Anger is a sinful passion that fails to produce God's righteousness, which is addressed by being quick to hear, slow to speak, and resolving wrath before the day ends to avoid sin. Scripture locates this struggle within worship and sanctification: people act from the heart, yet embodied weakness, habits, relationships, and circumstances can intensify temptation. The counselee remains an accountable image-bearer who needs both truthful compassion and the Spirit-enabled pattern of putting off sin, renewing the mind, and putting on Christlike love. Change rests on union with Christ and grace, not behavior management or self-salvation.",
    "topicId": "anger",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Your anger is telling you that something matters, but it cannot be trusted to judge the whole case or choose the right response. Some wrongs should grieve and provoke moral concern; nevertheless, intimidation, contempt, vengeance, harsh speech, and punishment belong to the old life, not to Christ’s way. God does not ask you to become indifferent. He calls you to place judgment in His hands and to pursue truth, protection, correction, and justice without becoming another source of evil.\n\nCome to Christ without defending the worst moment. Name whom you harmed, what you said or did, what you demanded, and what repair love now requires. His forgiveness is sufficient for specific guilt, and His Spirit can train a different response before the pressure disappears. If someone else’s sin is involved, you may set wise boundaries and seek proper help while refusing personal vengeance. Learn the strength of gentleness: pause before speaking, listen long enough to understand, tell the truth without contempt, and do active good where anger once sought to wound. Repentance is more than feeling bad after an explosion; it is a growing pattern of humility, self-control, restitution, and patient love that others can safely observe over time.",
