    "color": "purple",
    "steps": "Track symptoms, function, medicines, infections, side effects, appointments, and clinician-identified flare triggers without turning the log into self-surveillance. Ask the specialist for a flare and infection plan, vaccine guidance, pregnancy considerations where relevant, and clear thresholds for contact. Arrange flexible work, home, and church responsibilities around variable capacity. Read Psalm 13:1-2: How long, O LORD? 2 Corinthians 4:8-9: Afflicted in every way, but not crushed. Romans 12:12: Be patient in tribulation, be constant in prayer in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Autoimmune Disease + Flares",
    "icon": "🛡️",
    "scriptures": "Psalm 13:1-2: How long, O LORD? 2 Corinthians 4:8-9: Afflicted in every way, but not crushed. Romans 12:12: Be patient in tribulation, be constant in prayer.",
    "definition": "Autoimmune diseases occur when immune processes damage the body’s own tissues and may involve joints, skin, glands, blood, nerves, lungs, kidneys, or other organs. Symptoms and function can fluctuate, tests may not match how ill someone feels, and treatment may suppress immunity. Scripture provides durable language for waiting and affliction without blaming the sufferer, denying invisible symptoms, or portraying immune disease as a metaphor for spiritual rebellion. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "autoimmunedisease",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Unpredictable flares can make commitments feel risky and leave you repeatedly explaining why yesterday’s ability is not today’s. You may ask for flexibility without apologizing for symptoms you did not choose. Let the medical team manage immune treatment and let trusted believers provide practical continuity when capacity changes. God’s faithfulness is not measured by a smooth symptom chart, and lament need not be postponed until every test is conclusive.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Clarify the specific diagnosis or working diagnosis, organs affected, specialist plan, medicine risks, infection precautions, pain and fatigue, function, pregnancy concerns, employment, disbelief from others, and mental health. Avoid attributing flares to stress, sin, diet, or insufficient prayer without evidence, and do not recommend immune-altering supplements or restrictive regimens.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
