    "steps": "Record the exact cancer type and stage, treatment intent, options, schedule, likely side effects, fertility or sexuality concerns, emergency contact pathway, and supportive-care resources. Take a question list and note-taker to appointments; request help with transportation, meals, childcare, work, costs, and infection precautions; and agree on privacy boundaries before church communication. Read Psalm 27:13-14: I believe that I shall look upon the goodness of the LORD. Isaiah 43:2: When you pass through the waters, I will be with you in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Cancer + Treatment",
    "icon": "🎗️",
    "scriptures": "Psalm 27:13-14: I believe that I shall look upon the goodness of the LORD. Isaiah 43:2: When you pass through the waters, I will be with you. Romans 8:38-39: Nothing... will be able to separate us from the love of God.",
    "definition": "Cancer includes many diseases with different sites, stages, treatments, prognoses, and effects; the word itself does not supply an individual outcome. Diagnosis and treatment may involve surgery, radiation, medicines, surveillance, recurrence fears, body changes, fatigue, infertility, financial burden, and uncertainty. Scripture anchors the person in God’s inseparable love and permits lament without turning biblical hope into a prediction about remission. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "cancer",
    "pastoralGuidance": "You are more than a diagnosis, scan result, treatment response, or visible change in your body. Fear may return before appointments or after treatment ends, and gratitude can coexist with grief. Ask the oncology team direct questions, welcome palliative or supportive care at any stage, and resist unproven remedies that promise control while undermining treatment. The church can accompany you through ordinary presence, practical help, prayer, and willingness to hear hard news without rushing to explain it.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Clarify confirmed diagnosis and stage, treatment intent, symptoms, side effects, decision points, fertility concerns, finances, caregiving, mental health, spiritual interpretations, and the person’s desired level of disclosure. Coordinate with oncology social work, palliative care, chaplaincy, licensed counseling, and the local church while refusing causal blame or guarantees of healing.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
