    "color": "emerald",
    "steps": "Ask the person what assistance, language, equipment, seating, communication, transportation, sensory environment, and privacy they prefer. Request rehabilitation and accessibility evaluations where useful, document workplace or school accommodation needs, and audit church entrances, restrooms, platforms, classrooms, digital materials, emergency plans, and volunteer roles with the person’s leadership. Read John 9:3: It was not that this man sinned, or his parents. 1 Corinthians 12:22: The parts in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Disability + Changed Ability",
    "icon": "♿",
    "scriptures": "John 9:3: It was not that this man sinned, or his parents. 1 Corinthians 12:22: The parts... that seem to be weaker are indispensable. Luke 14:13: Invite the poor, the crippled, the lame, the blind.",
    "definition": "Disability may be present from birth or follow illness, injury, surgery, sensory loss, or neurological change, and it arises partly from the interaction between a person’s body and inaccessible environments. It may alter mobility, communication, energy, employment, privacy, and dependence without diminishing image-bearing dignity. Scripture rejects reflexive blame and requires the church to practice belonging and access, not merely offer sympathy from a distance. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "disability",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Your body or senses may work differently from what others expect, but you are not a lesson, project, or lesser member of Christ’s body. You may name grief and barriers while also refusing the assumption that disability makes life empty. Ask directly for access and correct people who speak around you rather than to you. The church should not require extraordinary courage simply to enter, communicate, participate, or serve.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Begin with the person’s own account of identity, function, goals, pain, fatigue, communication, assistance, discrimination, family dynamics, finances, sexuality, vocation, and church access. Separate requested help from paternalism, preserve consent, and coordinate rehabilitation, social services, legal accommodation resources, and mental-health care as appropriate.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
