    "color": "violet",
    "steps": "Acknowledge your struggle to believe God's promises and pray for help with your unbelief (Mark 9:24). Actively choose to remember and meditate on the deeds and past faithfulness of the Lord (Psalm 77:11). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in its literary context and summarize the truth about God, the human heart, and faithful obedience in your own words. Turn that truth into prayer, choose one observable practice for the week, invite a mature church member to accompany you, and bring both progress and remaining questions back for review.",
    "title": "Doubt + Faith",
    "icon": "❓",
    "scriptures": "Mark 9:24: I believe; help my unbelief! Psalm 77:11: I will remember the deeds of the LORD.",
    "definition": "Doubt may involve unanswered questions, weak assurance, suffering, temptation, incomplete understanding, or resistant unbelief; wise care discovers which struggle is actually present. Doubt is the internal struggle to believe God's promises and character, which is addressed by acknowledging one's unbelief through prayer and choosing to remember the Lord's past deeds and faithfulness. Scripture joins sound doctrine to lived discipleship. The mind must be renewed by truth understood in context, while faith works through love in concrete habits, relationships, worship, and church life. Growth is grounded in justification by faith, empowered by the Holy Spirit, ordinarily progressive rather than instantaneous, and directed toward conformity to Christ rather than image management or rule-keeping for acceptance.",
    "topicId": "doubt",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Doubt is not one undifferentiated enemy. Some questions arise from grief, intellectual confusion, disappointment, hypocrisy witnessed in the church, or truths never patiently explained. Other doubts become a shield against surrender. Jesus is not threatened by honest questions, and He does not ask you to manufacture certainty. He calls you to bring the question into the light, listen to trustworthy witnesses, and entrust yourself to Him even while understanding continues to grow.\n\nState the doubt precisely. Ask what evidence would actually matter, which assumptions are controlling the question, and whether a moral demand is hiding beneath an intellectual objection. Read Scripture in context rather than collecting isolated proof texts, and seek mature believers who will neither panic nor flatter unbelief. Remember that faith is not confidence in your own mental steadiness; it rests on the character, death, and resurrection of Christ. You may pray, “I believe; help my unbelief,” and keep walking in the light you have. Do not isolate until every problem is solved. Worship, obedience, fellowship, and patient study belong together. God often strengthens faith through the very process in which borrowed answers become examined and personally owned convictions.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Welcome honest questions, define the disputed claim, examine Scripture in context, distinguish faith from emotional certainty, address known disobedience, remember God’s works, and remain in worshiping community. Do not treat sincere questions as rebellion or offer slogans where careful exegesis, patience, lament, and intellectual honesty are needed. Pastoral method: identify the doctrinal claim, biblical text, habit, authority, community, and desired outcome involved in the struggle. Exegete the primary passages rather than treating a verse as a slogan, distinguish commands from wisdom and human preference, and connect the person’s standing in Christ to progressive Spirit-enabled obedience. Use instruction, questions, prayer, modeling, practice, and church relationships together. Assign work that demonstrates understanding and application, review it without creating dependence on the counselor, and measure maturity by growing truth, love, discernment, endurance, and teachability. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
