    "color": "yellow",
    "steps": "Ask the neurologist for a written seizure action plan covering seizure type, usual duration, first aid, rescue medicine, emergency thresholds, activity precautions, sleep, missed doses, and follow-up. Teach household and ministry leaders how to stay with the person, protect from injury, time the event, and preserve privacy. Review transportation and environmental safety without unnecessarily excluding the person. Read Psalm 121:3-4: He who keeps you will not slumber. Mark 9:22-25: If you can do anything, have compassion on us in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Epilepsy + Seizures",
    "icon": "⚡",
    "scriptures": "Psalm 121:3-4: He who keeps you will not slumber. Mark 9:22-25: If you can do anything, have compassion on us. Romans 12:10: Outdo one another in showing honor.",
    "definition": "Epilepsy involves a tendency toward recurrent seizures, which may look like convulsions, altered awareness, unusual movements, sensory changes, or brief confusion. The condition can affect driving, work, sleep, independence, safety, and social confidence, and it has sometimes been wrongly stigmatized as spiritual possession or moral failure. Scripture calls for compassion and honor; diagnosis and treatment belong to qualified neurological care, not speculative spiritual labeling. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "epilepsy",
    "pastoralGuidance": "A seizure can leave you vulnerable to embarrassment, lost time, injury, or fear that another episode will occur in public. You deserve care that protects both safety and dignity. Let trusted people learn your action plan, take medicines as directed, and discuss side effects or breakthrough events openly with your neurological team. Your condition is not evidence of weak faith or hidden spiritual contamination, and the church should never make spectacle of it.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Gather the established diagnosis, seizure description, triggers, typical recovery, medicine adherence and side effects, rescue plan, injuries, driving restrictions, sleep, mood, stigma, and the person’s wishes for disclosure. Ensure church workers know first aid while avoiding restraint, crowding, forced prayer, or assumptions about demonic causation.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
