    "color": "emerald",
    "steps": "Bring the gambling pattern and actual numbers into the light. List every form used—including sports betting, casinos, cards, lotteries, gaming wagers, speculative apps, or secret loans—along with dates, losses, debts, borrowed funds, unpaid necessities, tax exposure, and access to household money. Read Proverbs 13:11, 21:5, and 1 Timothy 6:6-10 in context, asking what quick gain promises and whose provision it risks. Stop access today by self-excluding where available, closing betting accounts, blocking transactions, surrendering control of shared funds to a safe and willing arrangement, and refusing new loans intended to chase losses. Tell affected family members through a supported plan and correct false financial statements. Contact a problem-gambling helpline or qualified clinician, and schedule debt, legal, or tax advice when consequences require it. Before the next conversation, create a complete debt and asset inventory, identify the emotional and situational path into each wager, establish a daily money check with a named helper, and choose one lawful act of work, restitution, or contented generosity. Set weekly review for the early months. If an urge or lapse occurs, do not attempt a final wager to repair it; disclose it immediately, protect remaining resources, and return to the plan.",
    "title": "Gambling + Financial Compulsion",
    "icon": "🎲",
    "scriptures": "Proverbs 13:11: Wealth gained hastily will dwindle, but whoever gathers little by little will increase it. Proverbs 21:5: The plans of the diligent lead surely to abundance, but everyone who is hasty comes only to poverty. 1 Timothy 6:6: Godliness with contentment is great gain.",
    "definition": "Problem gambling is a persistent pattern of wagering money or valuables despite growing harm, impaired control, or repeated efforts to recover losses through further risk. It can occur through sports books, casinos, lotteries, cards, online games, prediction markets, or speculative activity that functions as gambling, and it often hides behind excitement, false confidence, desperation, or the fantasy of rescuing a family through one win. Scripture does not reduce the concern to a single verse about chance; it addresses covetousness, stewardship, deceit, hasty gain, contentment, work, provision, and love of neighbor. The person remains accountable for choices while also needing practical interruption of a reinforced behavioral cycle. Biblical care should join repentance and hope with transparent finances, restricted access, family protection, professional help, and patient restitution.",
    "topicId": "gambling",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Gambling may have begun as entertainment, hope, competition, or escape, but once losses and secrecy grow it can make the next wager feel like the only way out. That is one of its most dangerous lies. Money already lost is not recovered by risking the rent, borrowing again, or hiding a larger bet. God’s care for you is not delivered through a private jackpot, and your worth is not waiting on a winning balance. You can stop chasing the past and tell the truth about today, even when the numbers bring painful consequences.\n\nOpen every account and obligation to a trustworthy helper. Name money taken from savings, household needs, employers, friends, credit, or retirement, and allow the real total to replace hopeful estimates. Accept barriers that prevent immediate access to betting and shared funds. These limits are not humiliation; they are practical love for you and for everyone whose future has been placed at risk. Seek specialized treatment because gambling can become a powerful learned cycle, and let financial, legal, or tax professionals address consequences outside a pastor’s competence. Do not promise a spouse that intensity alone proves change. Let steady truth, ordinary work, kept agreements, and restitution speak over time.\n\nContentment is not passive resignation to debt. It frees you to do the next honest task without demanding instant rescue: make the call, disclose the balance, cancel access, follow the budget, work faithfully, and receive help. If you have harmed others, repentance will include listening to their fear and accepting that they may protect resources independently. If you relapse, do not conceal it or chase the loss. Tell someone immediately and restore the barriers. Christ’s mercy can meet you in a financial story that cannot be quickly repaired, and His people can walk with you as patience, responsibility, generosity, and freedom from the wager grow little by little.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Gather a complete behavioral and financial history: forms of gambling, frequency, amounts, wins and losses, chasing, credit, loans, theft, tax liabilities, work effects, account access, prior abstinence, triggers, co-occurring substance use, mood shifts, and suicidal thinking. Verify numbers with consent rather than accepting an estimate shaped by shame or wishful memory. Determine whether dependents lack necessities, shared assets are endangered, fraud or crime occurred, or a spouse is being financially abused. Encourage immediate self-exclusion, transaction blocks, account closure, safe control of funds, and specialized clinical assessment. Pastors should not manage all household money themselves or create unaccountable dependency.\n\nPastoral method: trace the sequence from circumstance and body state through fantasy, access, wager, result, chasing, and concealment. Apply Scripture to worship, covetousness, stewardship, honest work, and contentment while also treating access and reinforcement as concrete habit factors. Build a plan for urges, paydays, sporting events, loneliness, and financial panic. Include restitution, debt counsel, truthful family communication, and weekly review of actual accounts. Coordinate with licensed gambling treatment, the national helpline, legal counsel, or financial professionals as indicated. Evaluate fruit through voluntary disclosure, protected necessities, absence of new secret debt, kept financial agreements, patient work, restored generosity, and endurance after losses—not through confidence, a short abstinence streak, or a final claim that the problem is solved.",
