    "color": "red",
    "steps": "Avoid overindulgence in food or drink by being mindful of your physical appetites (Proverbs 23:20-21). Ensure that whether you eat or drink, you do everything with the intention of bringing glory to God (1 Corinthians 10:31). Seek comfort in the Creator rather than the creation by relying on Him to satisfy your desires (1 Corinthians 10:31). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in its full paragraph, write the desire or fear that most often rules the moment, and name the specific lie it promises. Pray that truth back to God, remove one ordinary provision for the old pattern, practice one concrete opposite act of love, and report the result honestly to a mature believer.",
    "title": "Gluttony + Appetite",
    "icon": "🍽️",
    "scriptures": "Proverbs 23:20-21: Be not among drunkards or among gluttonous eaters of meat. 1 Corinthians 10:31: So, whether you eat or drink, or whatever you do, do all to the glory of God.",
    "definition": "Gluttony is not defined by body size but by a disordered relationship to appetite in which food, drink, comfort, or consumption exercises ruling control. Gluttony is the sin of overindulgence and a lack of self-control regarding physical appetites, where one's desire for food or material pleasure becomes an idol that takes priority over devotion to God. It is characterized by consuming to excess or with greedy eagerness, ultimately reflecting a heart that seeks satisfaction in the creation rather than the Creator. Scripture locates this struggle within worship and sanctification: people act from the heart, yet embodied weakness, habits, relationships, and circumstances can intensify temptation. The counselee remains an accountable image-bearer who needs both truthful compassion and the Spirit-enabled pattern of putting off sin, renewing the mind, and putting on Christlike love. Change rests on union with Christ and grace, not behavior management or self-salvation.",
    "topicId": "gluttony",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Food and appetite are gifts, but no appetite makes a faithful lord. Gluttony is not defined by appearance, a single meal, or another person’s preferred diet. It appears when comfort, quantity, secrecy, urgency, or self-indulgence repeatedly governs choices and dulls love for God and neighbor. Christ does not invite you into disgust for your body; He redeems embodied people and teaches them to receive good gifts with thanksgiving and self-control.\n\nBring the pattern into the light without crash vows or public shame. Learn when hunger is physical, emotional, social, or habitual, and receive medical or nutritional care when health conditions or disordered eating may be involved. Repentance may include planned meals, reduced access to particular triggers, eating with others, patient endurance of an unfulfilled craving, and replacing private soothing with prayer and relationship. Do not make thinness the gospel or turn disciplined eating into a new ground for pride. The aim is freedom to enjoy food without being mastered, to care for the body without worshiping it, and to remain available for love. Christ’s grace meets both failure and progress, training you to say no where needed and to receive daily bread as a grateful creature.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Assess patterns without appearance-based shame, identify cues and desired relief, address medical and nutritional realities, practice planned gratitude and restraint, remove provision for excess, and receive accountable support. Pastoral method: gather a concrete history of occasions, thoughts, desires, bodily conditions, access, habits, consequences, and relationships before assigning motives. Interpret the primary passages in their literary and canonical settings, connect indicatives of grace to imperatives of obedience, and formulate a specific put-off, mind-renewal, and put-on plan. Use the ordinary means of grace, accountable church relationships, environmental changes, and measurable practice while refusing speculative heart-reading. Review patterns over time, celebrate honest fruit, address concealment directly, and revise the plan when bodily or situational facts were missed. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
