    "color": "yellow",
    "steps": "Replace corrupting talk with words that are good for building up others (Ephesians 4:29). Refrain from whispering or spreading unnecessary information to avoid separating close friends (Proverbs 16:28). Use your speech to protect the reputation of others rather than tearing them down (Ephesians 4:29). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in context and prepare a factual account that separates what the other person did from your own interpretations and responses. Identify one responsibility that belongs to you, one matter that requires proper appeal or protection, and one truthful act of love you can take without pretending trust or reconciliation already exists.",
    "title": "Gossip + Slander",
    "icon": "🗣️",
    "scriptures": "Ephesians 4:29: Let no corrupting talk come out of your mouths, but only such as is good for building up. Proverbs 16:28: A whisperer separates close friends.",
    "definition": "Gossip and slander use speech to disclose, distort, or circulate another person’s life without a righteous need, often trading a neighbor’s reputation for belonging, influence, or self-justification. Gossip and slander involve the sinful use of speech to bear bad news behind someone's back out of a corrupt heart, characterized by breaking confidentiality or spreading corrupting talk that separates close friends rather than building others up. Scripture places this concern under the commands to love God and neighbor and understands relationships through truth, covenant faithfulness, justice, mercy, repentance, forgiveness, and wise boundaries. Each person remains responsible for his or her own conduct; another person’s sin may influence but never mechanically causes one’s response. Gospel reconciliation is precious, but it must be grounded in truth and cannot be manufactured by denying harm or bypassing repentance.",
    "topicId": "gossip",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Gossip offers belonging and influence by spending another person’s reputation. Even when the information is true, love asks whether you are the right person, speaking to the right person, for a righteous purpose, in a truthful and necessary way. Christ knows every word and still used His speech to give grace, defend the vulnerable, confront sin directly, and protect rather than consume reputations. He can make your mouth an instrument of the same kind of love.\n\nDo not excuse harmful speech as concern, venting, or prayer. Name what you repeated, why you wanted to tell it, and whom it affected. Where possible, correct false impressions, ask forgiveness, and stop the story from traveling further. If serious wrongdoing must be reported, speak to people with proper responsibility and preserve evidence; silence is not love when others need protection. In ordinary offenses, go toward the person rather than building a sympathetic audience. Learn to carry confidences within their lawful limits and to refuse conversations that have no redemptive purpose. Your identity does not require being the person who knows. As grace reshapes speech, you can become trustworthy—quick to listen, slow to accuse, courageous in direct truth, and generous toward the absent person.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Verify facts, identify whether the hearer needs to know, stop unnecessary circulation, confess to affected people, correct false impressions as widely as they spread, and replace corrosive speech with truthful protection and direct appeal. Confidential reporting of abuse, crime, danger, or misconduct to responsible authorities is not gossip and must not be suppressed under that label. Pastoral method: hear each person carefully without assuming equal power, equal knowledge, or equal culpability. Establish facts, chronology, promises, authority, patterns, witnesses, and consequences; then distinguish suffering, weakness, misunderstanding, and demonstrable sin. Interpret the primary passages in context and apply separate responsibilities rather than using a general command to pressure the more vulnerable person. Define confession, restitution, boundaries, trust, forgiveness, and reconciliation precisely, involve the local church appropriately, and evaluate change through observable fruit across time. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
