    "color": "gray",
    "steps": "Grieve with hope, knowing that in Christ, sorrow is not without a future (1 Thessalonians 4:13). Draw near to the Lord in your sorrow, trusting that He is close to those who are brokenhearted (Psalm 34:18). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in context and write a brief lament naming the loss, fear, bodily burden, and question you are carrying. Record one provision of God you can receive today, one necessary request for practical help, and one faithful responsibility small enough to undertake without pretending the suffering is resolved.",
    "title": "Grief + Loss",
    "icon": "🥀",
    "scriptures": "1 Thessalonians 4:13: That you may not grieve as others do who have no hope. Psalm 34:18: The LORD is near to the brokenhearted.",
    "definition": "Grief is the fitting sorrow of love encountering loss; Christian hope changes grief’s horizon without making tears, lament, remembrance, or bodily weakness faithless. Grief is the experience of sorrow and brokenheartedness that, for the believer, is marked by hope in Christ and the comfort of God’s presence rather than the despair of those without a future. Scripture distinguishes the evil a person suffers from the sins by which that person may respond. Suffering is not proof of personal guilt, and tears, weakness, bodily symptoms, or lament are not automatically unbelief. The sufferer remains God’s image-bearer, addressed as a whole embodied soul, offered Christ’s compassion and presence now, and anchored finally in resurrection, judgment, and the restoration of all things.",
    "topicId": "grief",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Grief is love bearing the pain of loss. You do not need to hurry past it, compare it, or turn it into a lesson before you have wept. Jesus stood at a grave and cried even though He knew resurrection was coming. Your tears do not deny hope; they testify that death and loss are enemies and that what God gave truly mattered. The Lord is near to the brokenhearted even when His nearness is not felt as comfort.\n\nLet grief have honest words in prayer, and let the church share its practical weight. Receive meals, company, silence, remembrance, and help with ordinary decisions. There is no faithful timetable for the disappearance of sorrow, and anniversaries may reopen it without erasing growth. At the same time, do not let grief seal you away from every living relationship and duty. In small measures, return to worship, embodied rhythms, service, and the people still given to you. If despair becomes incapacitating or thoughts of death emerge, seek immediate and sustained help. Christian hope does not mean you will stop missing what was lost. It means the risen Christ holds both the person who belongs to Him and the mourner who remains, and He will finally make all things new.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Learn the person and the loss, make room for lament, resist timetables, attend to sleep and daily care, remember with gratitude, pray the Psalms, receive the church’s presence, and take the next faithful responsibility. Watch for suicidal despair, prolonged inability to function, substance misuse, traumatic circumstances, or medical decline that requires additional skilled care. Pastoral method: begin with the person’s account of loss, threat, bodily experience, relationships, meaning, and present capacity. Distinguish lament from grumbling, weakness from rebellion, imposed shame from actual guilt, and suffering endured from sinful responses without forcing a predetermined ratio. Interpret the primary passages within the Bible’s creation-fall-redemption-consummation storyline, offer presence and practical mercy as well as words, and coordinate competent medical, legal, or civil help when the facts require it. Set modest goals, follow up reliably, and do not make visible emotional relief the measure of faithfulness. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
