    "color": "red",
    "steps": "Obtain the cardiology plan for medicines, activity, monitoring, diet, cardiac rehabilitation, follow-up, and symptoms that require emergency action. Keep a current medication list, arrange transportation and practical support, learn how to use prescribed devices, and ask when work, driving, exercise, and sexual activity may safely resume. Enroll in cardiac rehabilitation when offered. Read Psalm 31:14-15: I trust in you, O LORD. in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Heart Disease + Cardiac Recovery",
    "icon": "❤️‍🩹",
    "scriptures": "Psalm 31:14-15: I trust in you, O LORD... My times are in your hand. Proverbs 4:23: Keep your heart with all vigilance. Philippians 4:6: Let your requests be made known to God.",
    "definition": "Heart disease may involve coronary arteries, heart muscle, valves, rhythm, or pumping function and can bring acute danger alongside long-term treatment. After a heart attack or hospitalization, fear of recurrence may make ordinary sensations feel threatening, while rehabilitation and medicines alter daily life. Scripture invites vigilant stewardship and prayerful trust without confusing the biblical heart with the physical organ or treating anxiety as proof that symptoms are harmless. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "heartdisease",
    "pastoralGuidance": "A cardiac event can make you listen to every heartbeat and wonder whether normal life is safe. Bring those fears to both God and the medical team rather than choosing between prayer and evaluation. Follow the recovery plan, accept gradual activity, and allow trusted people to learn the emergency instructions. Your future is held by God, yet that truth supports rather than cancels medicines, rehabilitation, sleep, and the ordinary changes your clinicians recommend.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Gather the specific diagnosis, procedure, ejection or rhythm concerns if the patient understands them, medicines, rehabilitation referral, symptoms, emergency plan, mood, sleep, family fear, work, and access to follow-up. Distinguish reasonable vigilance from disabling anxiety while ensuring that pastoral reassurance never substitutes for assessment of new cardiac symptoms.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
