    "steps": "Ask the clinical team for the current problem list, goals for the day, warning signs, decision maker, and expected next update. Keep one shared log of names, questions, medicines, procedures, and decisions; arrange meals, transportation, childcare, lodging, and rest through a single church coordinator; and protect the patient’s privacy by agreeing who may receive updates. Read Psalm 23:4: Even though I walk through the valley. in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Hospitalization + Intensive Care",
    "icon": "🏥",
    "scriptures": "Psalm 23:4: Even though I walk through the valley... you are with me. Matthew 25:36: I was sick and you visited me. Romans 12:15: Weep with those who weep.",
    "definition": "Hospitalization removes ordinary routines and places a patient and family inside unfamiliar language, alarms, procedures, waiting, disrupted sleep, and limited control. Intensive care can add sedation, ventilation, delirium, rapid decisions, and uncertainty about survival or recovery. Scripture presents God’s presence in dark valleys and calls His people to embodied visitation, truthful lament, and practical burden-bearing rather than confident predictions about an outcome. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "hospitalizationicu",
    "pastoralGuidance": "The hospital may make time feel suspended while every sound seems important. You are allowed to receive one hour at a time rather than mastering the whole medical picture. God is present when prayer is fragmented, when the patient cannot speak, and when family members disagree or become exhausted. Let nurses, physicians, chaplains, social workers, therapists, and trusted believers carry their proper parts of the burden instead of expecting one relative to become the entire care system.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Clarify diagnosis, level of consciousness, capacity to make decisions, named surrogate, current clinical goals, visiting limitations, family communication, spiritual preferences, and the needs of children or vulnerable adults at home. Watch for delirium, traumatic stress, sleep deprivation, conflict around prognosis, and a family spokesperson who is becoming unable to function.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
