    "color": "indigo",
    "steps": "Recognize that as a believer, you are a new creation in Christ where the old has passed away and the new has come (2 Corinthians 5:17). Live with the understanding that you have been crucified with Christ and it is no longer you who live, but Christ who lives in you (Galatians 2:20). Base your foundational worth and identity on your union with Jesus rather than your own performance or the opinions of others (Galatians 2:20). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in its literary context and summarize the truth about God, the human heart, and faithful obedience in your own words. Turn that truth into prayer, choose one observable practice for the week, invite a mature church member to accompany you, and bring both progress and remaining questions back for review.",
    "title": "Identity in Christ",
    "icon": "🪞",
    "scriptures": "2 Corinthians 5:17: Therefore, if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation. The old has passed away; behold, the new has come. Galatians 2:20: I have been crucified with Christ. It is no longer I who live, but Christ who lives in me.",
    "definition": "Identity in Christ begins with union with Him and God’s objective verdict, calling, family, and future—not fluctuating achievement, desire, suffering, role, body, or another person’s approval. Identity in Christ is the state of being a new creation through union with Jesus, where one’s old life is replaced by Christ living within them, providing a foundational worth based on His work rather than personal performance. Scripture joins sound doctrine to lived discipleship. The mind must be renewed by truth understood in context, while faith works through love in concrete habits, relationships, worship, and church life. Growth is grounded in justification by faith, empowered by the Holy Spirit, ordinarily progressive rather than instantaneous, and directed toward conformity to Christ rather than image management or rule-keeping for acceptance.",
    "topicId": "identity",
    "pastoralGuidance": "You are more than your strongest feeling, worst failure, deepest wound, role, achievement, diagnosis, relationship status, or other people’s verdict. Those realities may describe important parts of your present experience, but they cannot bear the weight of naming you finally. If you belong to Christ, God’s decisive word is that you are united to His Son—forgiven, adopted, being renewed, and destined to share His resurrection life.\n\nIdentity in Christ does not erase creaturely particularity or make your story irrelevant. It puts every part of the story under a truer center. You can confess sin without becoming only “the failure,” acknowledge suffering without becoming only “the victim,” and receive gifts without making performance your worth. Learn to answer the question “Who am I?” with God’s acts and promises before turning to shifting self-perception. Then live from that belonging: put off what contradicts Christ, cultivate what resembles Him, receive your body and relationships as stewardship, and remain connected to His people. The goal is not endless inward analysis but a stable freedom to worship, love, work, suffer, and repent as someone whose life is hidden with Christ in God.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Identify the functional identity claim, study what is objectively true in Christ, lament losses without making them ultimate, repent of false masters, receive the church’s embodied belonging, and practice the vocation God gives today. Identity language should not erase biography, bodily suffering, ethnicity, vocation, or relationships; grace reorders these realities rather than pretending they do not matter. Pastoral method: identify the doctrinal claim, biblical text, habit, authority, community, and desired outcome involved in the struggle. Exegete the primary passages rather than treating a verse as a slogan, distinguish commands from wisdom and human preference, and connect the person’s standing in Christ to progressive Spirit-enabled obedience. Use instruction, questions, prayer, modeling, practice, and church relationships together. Assign work that demonstrates understanding and application, review it without creating dependence on the counselor, and measure maturity by growing truth, love, discernment, endurance, and teachability. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
