    "color": "zinc",
    "steps": "Keep yourself from idols by identifying and removing anything you love, trust, or obey more than the living God (1 John 5:21). Commit to having no other gods before Him, ensuring He remains the sole object of your worship and priority (Exodus 20:3). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in its full paragraph, write the desire or fear that most often rules the moment, and name the specific lie it promises. Pray that truth back to God, remove one ordinary provision for the old pattern, practice one concrete opposite act of love, and report the result honestly to a mature believer.",
    "title": "Idolatry + Worship",
    "icon": "🗿",
    "scriptures": "1 John 5:21: Little children, keep yourselves from idols. Exodus 20:3: You shall have no other gods before me.",
    "definition": "Idolatry gives created goods the trust, fear, love, obedience, or identity that belongs supremely to God, turning good desires into ruling demands. Idolatry is the act of placing anything above the living God in one's love, trust, or obedience, which is addressed by keeping oneself from idols and having no other gods before Him. Scripture locates this struggle within worship and sanctification: people act from the heart, yet embodied weakness, habits, relationships, and circumstances can intensify temptation. The counselee remains an accountable image-bearer who needs both truthful compassion and the Spirit-enabled pattern of putting off sin, renewing the mind, and putting on Christlike love. Change rests on union with Christ and grace, not behavior management or self-salvation.",
    "topicId": "idolatry",
    "pastoralGuidance": "An idol is not only a false religious image; it is any created good, desire, person, achievement, or refuge that receives the trust, fear, obedience, or hope owed to God. Idols promise life on terms you can manage, then demand sacrifice without giving rest. Christ exposes them not to leave you empty but to restore you to the living God, whose steadfast love is better than the gifts you have asked to save you.\n\nAsk what loss feels unbearable, what outcome seems necessary before you can obey, and where compromise appears reasonable because the desired thing is at stake. Name the rival honestly. Repentance includes turning from the practices that feed it and returning to worship, prayer, gratitude, generosity, and concrete love. Do not merely remove the object while leaving the heart’s promise untouched; learn why Christ is more trustworthy, beautiful, and sufficient. Created gifts can often be received again in their proper place—as gifts, not gods. Some attachments will require strong boundaries or permanent relinquishment. Freedom grows as you repeatedly choose obedience when the idol’s promise is loud and discover that God remains faithful. You were made not merely to avoid worshiping badly, but to delight in the One who made and redeemed you.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Trace recurring emotion and behavior to the desired treasure, name the promise the idol makes, expose its cost and impotence, behold God’s superior worth, remove provision, and practice opposite worship in community. Not every strong desire or grief is idolatry; counselors must avoid speculative heart-reading and establish conclusions from Scripture, fruit, and careful listening. Pastoral method: gather a concrete history of occasions, thoughts, desires, bodily conditions, access, habits, consequences, and relationships before assigning motives. Interpret the primary passages in their literary and canonical settings, connect indicatives of grace to imperatives of obedience, and formulate a specific put-off, mind-renewal, and put-on plan. Use the ordinary means of grace, accountable church relationships, environmental changes, and measurable practice while refusing speculative heart-reading. Review patterns over time, celebrate honest fruit, address concealment directly, and revise the plan when bodily or situational facts were missed. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
