    "color": "lime",
    "steps": "Recognize that where jealousy and selfish ambition exist, there will be disorder and every vile practice (James 3:16). Acknowledge the destructive power of jealousy, which can be more overwhelming than wrath or anger (Proverbs 27:4). Turn from resentful longing and suspicious fear toward a pursuit of peace and godly order (James 3:16). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in its full paragraph, write the desire or fear that most often rules the moment, and name the specific lie it promises. Pray that truth back to God, remove one ordinary provision for the old pattern, practice one concrete opposite act of love, and report the result honestly to a mature believer.",
    "title": "Jealousy",
    "icon": "😒",
    "scriptures": "James 3:16: For where jealousy and selfish ambition exist, there will be disorder and every vile practice. Proverbs 27:4: Wrath is cruel, anger is overwhelming, but who can stand before jealousy?",
    "definition": "Jealousy can name a fitting concern for covenant faithfulness or a sinful demand to possess, monitor, or control another person; the object, standard, and fruit reveal the difference. Jealousy is a destructive and sinful passion characterized by selfish ambition and resentment toward the advantages or blessings of others, which leads to disorder and every vile practice. It is regarded as a powerful force more overwhelming than wrath or anger, requiring a heart turned toward God's peace and order to overcome. Scripture locates this struggle within worship and sanctification: people act from the heart, yet embodied weakness, habits, relationships, and circumstances can intensify temptation. The counselee remains an accountable image-bearer who needs both truthful compassion and the Spirit-enabled pattern of putting off sin, renewing the mind, and putting on Christlike love. Change rests on union with Christ and grace, not behavior management or self-salvation.",
    "topicId": "jealousy",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Jealousy fears that another person’s attention, affection, opportunity, or gift diminishes your own place. It can make suspicion feel like discernment and control feel like love. God’s covenant jealousy is holy because He knows perfectly and loves rightly; ours is often mixed with insecurity, possession, and imagined entitlement. Christ offers security that does not require monitoring another person or competing for a verdict of worth.\n\nName what you fear losing and what evidence actually exists. If trust has been broken, seek truth, appropriate transparency, and wise boundaries rather than being shamed for every concern. If the fear rests mainly on comparison or imagination, confess the accusations, tests, manipulation, or surveillance it has produced. Love does not demand omniscience. Learn to ask directly, listen without prosecuting, and allow another person to have relationships and gifts that are not a rejection of you. Your deepest belonging is secured in Christ, which frees you to pursue faithfulness without controlling outcomes. Trust may need to be rebuilt by observable fruit, but jealousy itself cannot build it. As you rest in God’s knowledge and care, you can become more truthful about danger, less ruled by suspicion, and more able to seek another person’s good.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Clarify the threatened relationship, test suspicions against facts, distinguish covenant concern from ownership, confess control and accusation, pursue truthful conversation, and rebuild warranted trust through observable faithfulness. Pastoral method: gather a concrete history of occasions, thoughts, desires, bodily conditions, access, habits, consequences, and relationships before assigning motives. Interpret the primary passages in their literary and canonical settings, connect indicatives of grace to imperatives of obedience, and formulate a specific put-off, mind-renewal, and put-on plan. Use the ordinary means of grace, accountable church relationships, environmental changes, and measurable practice while refusing speculative heart-reading. Review patterns over time, celebrate honest fruit, address concealment directly, and revise the plan when bodily or situational facts were missed. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
