    "color": "blue",
    "steps": "Write down kidney function questions, medicines, dietary and fluid guidance, access-care instructions, dialysis schedule, transplant evaluation steps, and symptoms that require contact. Ask for a renal dietitian and social worker, plan transportation and rest after treatment, protect the access exactly as instructed, and discuss treatment options early enough for informed decisions. Read Isaiah 46:4: I have made, and I will bear; I will carry and will save. Psalm 55:22: Cast your burden on the LORD in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Kidney Disease + Dialysis",
    "icon": "💧",
    "scriptures": "Isaiah 46:4: I have made, and I will bear; I will carry and will save. Psalm 55:22: Cast your burden on the LORD. Galatians 6:2: Bear one another’s burdens.",
    "definition": "Chronic kidney disease can progress quietly and may eventually require dialysis, transplantation, or conservative kidney care. Treatment can reshape food and fluid choices, work, travel, energy, finances, and family schedules, while access procedures and laboratory results add repeated vulnerability. Scripture calls the church to sustained burden-bearing and truthful hope without presenting one treatment choice as the universal measure of faith. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "kidneydisease",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Dialysis and kidney care can consume hours that others never see. You may grieve spontaneity, familiar foods, strength, or the sense that your body works without machinery. Receive specialized help and make treatment decisions with accurate information, prayer, and your values clearly heard. Dependence on a machine, donor, caregiver, or schedule does not make your life less meaningful or your service to Christ less real.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Gather disease stage as the patient understands it, treatment options, dialysis experience, access condition, medicines, dietary burden, fatigue, work, transport, finances, transplant hopes, family conflict, and emotional health. Encourage nephrology, renal nutrition, social work, and palliative input; do not prescribe fluid, potassium, protein, supplements, or dialysis choices.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
