    "steps": "Husbands should love their wives sacrificially, just as Christ loved the church and gave Himself up for her (Ephesians 5:25). Each husband must love his wife as himself, and each wife must ensure that she respects her husband (Ephesians 5:33). Maintain the marriage as a lifelong covenant that displays the mystery of Christ's love for His church through mutual sacrificial love and respect (Ephesians 5:25, 33). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in context and prepare a factual account that separates what the other person did from your own interpretations and responses. Identify one responsibility that belongs to you, one matter that requires proper appeal or protection, and one truthful act of love you can take without pretending trust or reconciliation already exists.",
    "title": "Marriage + Covenant",
    "icon": "💍",
    "scriptures": "Ephesians 5:25: Husbands, love your wives, as Christ loved the church and gave himself up for her. Ephesians 5:33: Let each one of you love his wife as himself, and let the wife see that she respects her husband.",
    "definition": "Marriage is a one-flesh covenant ordered toward faithful companionship, holiness, fruitfulness, and a living portrayal of Christ’s covenant love for His church. Marriage is a lifelong covenant that mirrors the relationship between Christ and the church, fulfilled through a husband's sacrificial love and a wife's respect. Scripture places this concern under the commands to love God and neighbor and understands relationships through truth, covenant faithfulness, justice, mercy, repentance, forgiveness, and wise boundaries. Each person remains responsible for his or her own conduct; another person’s sin may influence but never mechanically causes one’s response. Gospel reconciliation is precious, but it must be grounded in truth and cannot be manufactured by denying harm or bypassing repentance.",
    "topicId": "marriage",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Marriage is a covenant in which two sinners are called to a shared life of faithful, sacrificial love under Christ. It is not sustained by chemistry alone, nor by one spouse carrying every responsibility while the other demands submission without service. God’s design dignifies both husband and wife and makes authority accountable to the self-giving pattern of Jesus. Ordinary weakness calls for patience; entrenched deceit, coercion, adultery, abandonment, or abuse must be named and addressed rather than relabeled as communication difficulty.\n\nBring your own vows and conduct before God before building the case against your spouse. Confess specifically, listen seriously, keep lawful promises, and practice concrete love when emotion is weak. Seek help early rather than waiting for contempt and secrecy to harden. Forgiveness can begin before trust is restored, but reconciliation requires truth and observable fruit from each responsible person. If danger or coercive control is present, pursue safety and qualified intervention before joint counseling. The goal is not preserving an image at any cost; it is a marriage that increasingly tells the truth about Christ’s covenant love. Where that shared work is possible, commit to patient repair. Where a spouse persistently repudiates covenant life, let mature shepherds help discern protection, discipline, and biblical options without pressuring the innocent spouse to manufacture peace.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Hear both spouses without forced symmetry, establish patterns, address each person’s sin and suffering, restore truthful communication, repentance, forgiveness, affection, stewardship, and shared worship, and measure change over time. Pastoral method: hear each person carefully without assuming equal power, equal knowledge, or equal culpability. Establish facts, chronology, promises, authority, patterns, witnesses, and consequences; then distinguish suffering, weakness, misunderstanding, and demonstrable sin. Interpret the primary passages in context and apply separate responsibilities rather than using a general command to pressure the more vulnerable person. Define confession, restitution, boundaries, trust, forgiveness, and reconciliation precisely, involve the local church appropriately, and evaluate change through observable fruit across time. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
