    "scriptures": "Psalm 139:1-3: O LORD, you have searched me and known me. James 1:5: If any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask God. Matthew 6:34: Sufficient for the day is its own trouble.",
    "definition": "A new diagnosis can reorder a person’s picture of the future within a single conversation, while an incomplete diagnosis can leave symptoms real but their name, course, and treatment uncertain. Shock may impair memory and invite catastrophic assumptions or premature certainty. Scripture gives room for honest questions, daily dependence, wise counsel, and confidence in God’s character without claiming that every prognosis will improve or that every unanswered question has an immediate spiritual explanation. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "newdiagnosis",
    "pastoralGuidance": "You do not have to absorb an entire future on the day a condition is named. Shock, tears, numbness, or the need to hear information again are human responses, not verdicts on your trust in God. Separate today’s facts from feared possibilities, allow clinicians to revise conclusions as evidence develops, and bring each actual decision into prayer and wise counsel. Hope in Christ is sturdier than optimism because it remains true when a diagnosis is serious and when medicine cannot promise an outcome.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
