    "color": "emerald",
    "steps": "- Train and instruct your children in the ways of the Lord to avoid provoking them to anger (Ephesians 6:4) - Diligently teach God's Word to your children throughout the day, whether at home or traveling (Deuteronomy 6:7) - Model the discipline and instruction of the Lord as a steward of His grace (Ephesians 6:4) Understand the child’s age and capacities, align parents where possible, distinguish rebellion from weakness, give clear instruction, correct proportionately, repair parental sin, reinforce good, and cultivate relationship and worship. End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in context and prepare a factual account that separates what the other person did from your own interpretations and responses. Identify one responsibility that belongs to you, one matter that requires proper appeal or protection, and one truthful act of love you can take without pretending trust or reconciliation already exists.",
    "title": "Parenting + Family",
    "icon": "👨‍👩‍👧‍👦",
    "scriptures": "Ephesians 6:4: Fathers, do not provoke your children to anger, but bring them up in the discipline and instruction of the Lord. Deuteronomy 6:7: You shall teach them diligently to your children, and shall talk of them when you sit in your house...",
    "definition": "Parenting is delegated stewardship that combines instruction, example, nurture, correction, protection, and increasing preparation for a child’s responsible maturity before God. Parenting is the stewardship of children by bringing them up in the discipline and instruction of the Lord while diligently teaching them His Word and avoiding provocation to anger. Scripture places this concern under the commands to love God and neighbor and understands relationships through truth, covenant faithfulness, justice, mercy, repentance, forgiveness, and wise boundaries. Each person remains responsible for his or her own conduct; another person’s sin may influence but never mechanically causes one’s response. Gospel reconciliation is precious, but it must be grounded in truth and cannot be manufactured by denying harm or bypassing repentance.",
    "topicId": "parenting",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Your child is an image-bearer entrusted to your care, not a project whose performance proves your worth. Parenting joins warm affection, clear instruction, fitting discipline, protection, prayer, and an example of repentance. You cannot regenerate a child’s heart, control every outcome, or guarantee that faithful work will produce the story you imagined. You can represent God’s truth and character with consistency while entrusting the results to Him.\n\nDo not discipline mainly to end embarrassment, inconvenience, or dissent. Understand what happened, distinguish immaturity from defiance, make expectations plain, and keep consequences proportionate and free from rage, humiliation, threats, or physical harm. When you sin against your child, confess without blaming the child for provoking you; parental repentance teaches the gospel rather than weakening authority. Adapt care to age, ability, health, and particular needs. If your child is endangered, self-harming, abused, or showing serious medical or developmental concerns, seek qualified help promptly. In ordinary life, give more than correction: listen, delight, worship, work, eat, and serve together. Your child needs a parent who depends on Christ, not one who performs perfection. Faithfulness is measured by loving obedience in your role, not by controlling another person’s final choices.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Understand the child’s age and capacities, align parents where possible, distinguish rebellion from weakness, give clear instruction, correct proportionately, repair parental sin, reinforce good, and cultivate relationship and worship. Pastoral method: hear each person carefully without assuming equal power, equal knowledge, or equal culpability. Establish facts, chronology, promises, authority, patterns, witnesses, and consequences; then distinguish suffering, weakness, misunderstanding, and demonstrable sin. Interpret the primary passages in context and apply separate responsibilities rather than using a general command to pressure the more vulnerable person. Define confession, restitution, boundaries, trust, forgiveness, and reconciliation precisely, involve the local church appropriately, and evaluate change through observable fruit across time. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
