    "color": "sky",
    "steps": "Shepherd the flock of God by being a godly example to those under your care (1 Peter 5:2-3). Seek the lost and bring back the strayed, reflecting God's own heart as the Good Shepherd (Ezekiel 34:15-16). Provide tender and attentive leadership, especially during times of crisis (1 Peter 5:2). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in context and write a brief lament naming the loss, fear, bodily burden, and question you are carrying. Record one provision of God you can receive today, one necessary request for practical help, and one faithful responsibility small enough to undertake without pretending the suffering is resolved.",
    "title": "Pastoral Care Needs",
    "icon": "🐑",
    "scriptures": "1 Peter 5:2-3: Shepherd the flock of God that is among you... being examples to the flock. Ezekiel 34:15-16: I myself will be the shepherd of my sheep... I will seek the lost, and I will bring back the strayed...",
    "definition": "Pastoral care is the church’s personal ministry of Scripture, prayer, presence, protection, practical help, correction, and patient accompaniment through sin and suffering. Pastoral care is the ministry of shepherding God's flock by providing spiritual, emotional, and practical support, modeled after the Good Shepherd who seeks the lost and cares for His sheep. It involves leading through godly example, offering tender leadership during crises, and drawing individuals toward the mercy and restorative grace of God. Scripture distinguishes the evil a person suffers from the sins by which that person may respond. Suffering is not proof of personal guilt, and tears, weakness, bodily symptoms, or lament are not automatically unbelief. The sufferer remains God’s image-bearer, addressed as a whole embodied soul, offered Christ’s compassion and presence now, and anchored finally in resurrection, judgment, and the restoration of all things.",
    "topicId": "pastoralcare",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Needing pastoral care does not mean you have failed at Christian maturity. Christ gives shepherds and a body because believers are not meant to carry every sorrow, decision, sin struggle, conflict, or season of weakness alone. You may come before you have organized the story or identified the perfect request. A faithful pastor should listen, open Scripture carefully, pray, help distinguish responsibilities, and connect words with concrete care.\n\nPastoral care also has limits. A pastor is not omniscient, cannot promise secrecy when someone is in danger, and should not work outside competence or without accountability. You may ask how confidentiality works, who will be involved, what records are kept, and when medical, legal, or specialized help is needed. If a shepherd uses access to control, isolate, threaten, solicit inappropriate intimacy, or protect wrongdoing, seek help outside that leader’s authority. In healthy care, the aim is not dependence on one gifted person but deeper dependence on Christ and fuller participation in His church. Bring the truth, remain teachable, test counsel by Scripture, and allow others to bear practical burdens. You are not an interruption to the church’s ministry; caring for souls is part of what the church is for.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Clarify the person’s request, listen before assigning a category, assess urgency and safety, connect biblical truth to the actual experience, define the next step, mobilize appropriate church support, and follow up. Pastoral method: begin with the person’s account of loss, threat, bodily experience, relationships, meaning, and present capacity. Distinguish lament from grumbling, weakness from rebellion, imposed shame from actual guilt, and suffering endured from sinful responses without forcing a predetermined ratio. Interpret the primary passages within the Bible’s creation-fall-redemption-consummation storyline, offer presence and practical mercy as well as words, and coordinate competent medical, legal, or civil help when the facts require it. Set modest goals, follow up reliably, and do not make visible emotional relief the measure of faithfulness. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
