    "color": "cyan",
    "steps": "Acknowledge that no one on earth is perfectly righteous or without sin (Ecclesiastes 7:20). Rely on God's grace as sufficient for your weaknesses, allowing His power to be made perfect in your imperfections (2 Corinthians 12:9). Shift your focus from flawless performance to trusting in God's strength (2 Corinthians 12:9). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in its full paragraph, write the desire or fear that most often rules the moment, and name the specific lie it promises. Pray that truth back to God, remove one ordinary provision for the old pattern, practice one concrete opposite act of love, and report the result honestly to a mature believer.",
    "title": "Perfectionism",
    "icon": "📏",
    "scriptures": "Ecclesiastes 7:20: Surely there is not a righteous man on earth who does good and never sins. 2 Corinthians 12:9: But he said to me, 'My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness.'",
    "definition": "Perfectionism binds worth, safety, or acceptance to flawless control and performance, often producing anxiety, procrastination, harshness, concealment, and inability to receive creaturely limits. Perfectionism is the unrealistic pursuit of flawless performance and the denial of human fallibility, which is addressed by relying on God's sufficient grace and finding strength in His power made perfect through our weaknesses. Scripture locates this struggle within worship and sanctification: people act from the heart, yet embodied weakness, habits, relationships, and circumstances can intensify temptation. The counselee remains an accountable image-bearer who needs both truthful compassion and the Spirit-enabled pattern of putting off sin, renewing the mind, and putting on Christlike love. Change rests on union with Christ and grace, not behavior management or self-salvation.",
    "topicId": "perfectionism",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Perfectionism promises safety through flawless performance, but it keeps moving the standard and turns mistakes into verdicts on your worth. Excellence can be an expression of love; perfectionism makes excellence a master, uses fear and shame as fuel, and often burdens other people with standards they were never asked to carry. Christ calls you to faithful stewardship, not omniscience, limitless capacity, or sinless achievement in this age.\n\nReceive your limits as part of being a creature. Define what faithfulness requires for the actual task, not the imagined standard that would prevent every criticism. Work carefully, seek counsel, and then allow appropriate work to be finished. When you fail or are corrected, confess real sin and learn without converting the event into total self-condemnation. Grace does not make effort unimportant; it frees effort from being your justification. Notice where perfectionism produces delay, control, irritability, avoidance, or neglect of people, and practice a more loving measure. Others need your honest presence more than your polished image. Because Christ’s righteousness, not your record, secures your welcome with God, you can risk learning, delegate, rest, and offer imperfect but sincere service with gratitude.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Identify the feared consequence of imperfection, distinguish faithfulness from omnipotence, define a truthful standard of completion, expose avoidance, confess judgment, receive grace, and practice finishing limited work openly. High standards and careful craftsmanship are not themselves sinful; counsel should address ruling fear and false judgment rather than punish diligence. Pastoral method: gather a concrete history of occasions, thoughts, desires, bodily conditions, access, habits, consequences, and relationships before assigning motives. Interpret the primary passages in their literary and canonical settings, connect indicatives of grace to imperatives of obedience, and formulate a specific put-off, mind-renewal, and put-on plan. Use the ordinary means of grace, accountable church relationships, environmental changes, and measurable practice while refusing speculative heart-reading. Review patterns over time, celebrate honest fruit, address concealment directly, and revise the plan when bodily or situational facts were missed. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
