    "color": "rose",
    "steps": "Keep prenatal and specialist contacts, medicines, blood-pressure or glucose instructions when prescribed, hospital route, childcare, transportation, and warning signs accessible. Ask how the complication affects activity, work, delivery planning, fetal monitoring, and postpartum care. Bring a support person to complex appointments and record questions without relying on internet anecdotes. Read Psalm 139:13: You knitted me together in my mother’s womb. Isaiah 66:13: As one whom his mother comforts, so I will comfort you in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Pregnancy Complications + Maternal Health",
    "icon": "🤰",
    "scriptures": "Psalm 139:13: You knitted me together in my mother’s womb. Isaiah 66:13: As one whom his mother comforts, so I will comfort you. Psalm 34:18: The LORD is near to the brokenhearted.",
    "definition": "Pregnancy complications can affect the pregnant person, fetus, or both and may involve high blood pressure, bleeding, diabetes, infection, preterm labor, placental problems, severe nausea, or other acute concerns. Even previously healthy pregnancies can become high risk. Scripture honors embodied life and compassionate care without assigning blame, promising a particular outcome, or treating maternal safety as secondary. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "pregnancycomplications",
    "pastoralGuidance": "A complicated pregnancy can hold joy, fear, grief, and bodily exhaustion at the same time. You did not create danger by asking hard questions or by needing medicine, monitoring, hospitalization, or a changed birth plan. Receive skilled obstetric care promptly and let trusted people reduce practical burdens. Hope does not require you to call uncertainty safe or to hide the possibility of loss.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Clarify gestational stage, confirmed complication, obstetric instructions, present symptoms, prior losses or trauma, medicines, support, transportation, childcare, work, birth expectations, and mental health. Protect privacy, use qualified obstetric and maternal-fetal medicine input, and do not offer causal theories, medication advice, or pressure around delivery method.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
