    "color": "purple",
    "steps": "Recognize that pride leads to destruction and a haughty spirit precedes a fall (Proverbs 16:18). Humble yourself before God to receive His grace, as He opposes the proud but gives grace to the humble (James 4:6). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in its full paragraph, write the desire or fear that most often rules the moment, and name the specific lie it promises. Pray that truth back to God, remove one ordinary provision for the old pattern, practice one concrete opposite act of love, and report the result honestly to a mature believer.",
    "title": "Pride + Arrogance",
    "icon": "🦚",
    "scriptures": "Proverbs 16:18: Pride goes before destruction, and a haughty spirit before a fall. James 4:6: God opposes the proud but gives grace to the humble.",
    "definition": "Pride interprets self, gifts, knowledge, suffering, or status independently of God, seeking elevation, control, or exemption from the humble dependence owed to the Creator. Pride is the state of having a haughty spirit that leads to destruction, which is addressed by humbling oneself before God to receive His grace instead of His opposition. Scripture locates this struggle within worship and sanctification: people act from the heart, yet embodied weakness, habits, relationships, and circumstances can intensify temptation. The counselee remains an accountable image-bearer who needs both truthful compassion and the Spirit-enabled pattern of putting off sin, renewing the mind, and putting on Christlike love. Change rests on union with Christ and grace, not behavior management or self-salvation.",
    "topicId": "pride",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Pride places the self at the center—above correction, hungry for recognition, preoccupied with comparison, or quietly convinced that weakness must remain hidden. It can boast loudly or condemn itself dramatically; both keep attention fixed on self. Christ’s humility does not erase dignity. He frees you to receive every gift as grace, every limitation as creaturely truth, and every person as a neighbor rather than an audience or competitor.\n\nAsk how pride appears when you are overlooked, opposed, corrected, or dependent. Confess the specific defensiveness, contempt, exaggeration, control, or refusal to listen. Then practice humility in embodied ways: ask questions, admit what you do not know, receive help, honor another person’s contribution, and serve where no recognition is likely. Do not announce humility or turn self-hatred into virtue. Look outward to the glory of God and the good of others. If you hold authority, use it to protect and equip rather than to insulate yourself from challenge. The gospel leaves no room for superiority and no need for despair: you were helpless enough to need the cross and loved enough that Christ gave Himself for you. That truth can make you both lowly and secure.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Notice boasting, defensiveness, comparison, blame, and resistance to correction; confess specifically, receive accurate feedback, give thanks, honor others, serve without recognition, and remember every good gift is received. Humility is truthful creatureliness, not accepting false accusations, denying gifts, enabling mistreatment, or surrendering responsibilities God actually assigned. Pastoral method: gather a concrete history of occasions, thoughts, desires, bodily conditions, access, habits, consequences, and relationships before assigning motives. Interpret the primary passages in their literary and canonical settings, connect indicatives of grace to imperatives of obedience, and formulate a specific put-off, mind-renewal, and put-on plan. Use the ordinary means of grace, accountable church relationships, environmental changes, and measurable practice while refusing speculative heart-reading. Review patterns over time, celebrate honest fruit, address concealment directly, and revise the plan when bodily or situational facts were missed. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
