    "color": "amber",
    "steps": "Abstain from sexual immorality, recognizing that it is the will of God for your life (1 Thessalonians 4:3-4). Guard your heart and mind by refusing to look at others with lustful intent (Matthew 5:28). Seek to honor God's design for intimacy by exercising self-control over your own body (1 Thessalonians 4:4). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in its full paragraph, write the desire or fear that most often rules the moment, and name the specific lie it promises. Pray that truth back to God, remove one ordinary provision for the old pattern, practice one concrete opposite act of love, and report the result honestly to a mature believer.",
    "title": "Purity + Lust",
    "icon": "👁️",
    "scriptures": "1 Thessalonians 4:3-4: This is the will of God... that you abstain from sexual immorality. Matthew 5:28: Everyone who looks at a woman with lustful intent has already committed adultery with her in his heart.",
    "definition": "Sexual purity orders desire, imagination, body, and relationship under God’s design, seeking another person’s holiness rather than consuming an image-bearer for private gratification. Purity is the commitment to abstain from sexual immorality and lustful intent in accordance with God's will, honoring His design for intimacy through self-control of the heart and body. Scripture locates this struggle within worship and sanctification: people act from the heart, yet embodied weakness, habits, relationships, and circumstances can intensify temptation. The counselee remains an accountable image-bearer who needs both truthful compassion and the Spirit-enabled pattern of putting off sin, renewing the mind, and putting on Christlike love. Change rests on union with Christ and grace, not behavior management or self-salvation.",
    "topicId": "purity",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Sexual sin promises intimacy, relief, excitement, or control while training desire away from covenant love and the dignity of persons. Shame then urges further secrecy. Christ offers neither casual permission nor hopeless condemnation. He calls sexual sin what it is, receives those who confess, and gives His Spirit so that body and imagination can be retrained for holiness, honor, and genuine love.\n\nBring the whole pattern into the light with mature, same-sex accountability. Remove access that turns temptation into a private routine, and do not keep a cherished doorway while asking God for freedom. Learn the circumstances, fantasies, resentments, loneliness, entitlement, and habits that feed the cycle. Repentance includes what you pursue as well as what you avoid: worship, honest friendship, meaningful work, service, sleep, and a view of people as image-bearers rather than instruments. If your conduct involved another person, coercion, illegal material, exploitation, or betrayal, protection, reporting, restitution, and serious consequences may be required. Marriage is not a cure for lust, and singleness is not impurity. Your body belongs to the Lord, and holiness is not the destruction of desire but its truthful ordering under love.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Assess secrecy, access, triggers, escalation, coercion, and affected relationships; confess fully to appropriate people, remove provision, establish device and relational accountability, renew the mind, and practice honorable embodied love. Pastoral method: gather a concrete history of occasions, thoughts, desires, bodily conditions, access, habits, consequences, and relationships before assigning motives. Interpret the primary passages in their literary and canonical settings, connect indicatives of grace to imperatives of obedience, and formulate a specific put-off, mind-renewal, and put-on plan. Use the ordinary means of grace, accountable church relationships, environmental changes, and measurable practice while refusing speculative heart-reading. Review patterns over time, celebrate honest fruit, address concealment directly, and revise the plan when bodily or situational facts were missed. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
