    "steps": "Create a concise medical timeline with symptoms, objective findings, tests, treatments and responses, family history, records, imaging, and current clinicians. Ask the primary clinician to coordinate referrals and symptom management, seek appropriate academic or rare-disease expertise, verify information sources, and prepare focused questions so that each consultation builds on prior work. Read Psalm 13:2: How long must I take counsel in my soul? Proverbs 18:13: If one gives an answer before he hears, it is his folly. Romans 8:26: The Spirit helps us in our weakness in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Rare + Undiagnosed Illness",
    "icon": "🔎",
    "scriptures": "Psalm 13:2: How long must I take counsel in my soul? Proverbs 18:13: If one gives an answer before he hears, it is his folly. Romans 8:26: The Spirit helps us in our weakness.",
    "definition": "Rare and undiagnosed illnesses can involve years of symptoms, inconclusive tests, changing labels, few specialists, travel, financial strain, and repeated experiences of not being believed. Absence of a diagnosis does not prove absence of disease, and a rare diagnosis may still have limited treatment evidence. Scripture commends careful listening, humility about what is unknown, and companionship in prolonged uncertainty rather than confident causal claims. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "rareundiagnosedillness",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Not having a name for what is happening can make you feel invisible or trapped between hope and exhaustion. You may continue seeking competent evaluation without making the search your entire identity, and you may receive symptom care before every cause is known. God does not require you to invent certainty. Let trusted people help organize records, travel, meals, and prayer while respecting the limits of what anyone can promise.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Listen to the full timeline, functional effects, prior medical experiences, current coordination, costs, travel, family history, online communities, mental health, and fears of dismissal. Avoid diagnosing, endorsing unverified tests, or implying symptoms are spiritual or psychological because standard evaluation is inconclusive; support careful specialist consultation and integrated symptom care.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
