    "color": "lime",
    "steps": "Ask the rehabilitation team for functional goals, home exercises, equipment, precautions, caregiver training, follow-up, and signs of overexertion or medical concern. Build practice into a sustainable schedule with rest, transport, nutrition, and accessible church participation. Track meaningful function rather than comparing recovery with another person’s timeline. Read Zechariah 4:10: Do not despise the day of small things. Hebrews 12:12: Lift your drooping hands and strengthen your weak knees in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Rehabilitation + Long Recovery",
    "icon": "🪜",
    "scriptures": "Zechariah 4:10: Do not despise the day of small things. Hebrews 12:12: Lift your drooping hands and strengthen your weak knees. Philippians 1:6: He who began a good work in you will bring it to completion.",
    "definition": "Rehabilitation after illness, injury, stroke, surgery, or prolonged hospitalization seeks to restore or adapt movement, speech, cognition, swallowing, endurance, and daily function. Progress may be slow, nonlinear, and incomplete, and insurance or transportation can limit access. Scripture values patient faithfulness in small things while refusing to measure a person’s spiritual maturity by the speed or extent of physical recovery. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "rehabilitation",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Repeating a small task can feel far removed from the life you want back, yet patient work may be genuine progress even when it is hard to see. Receive skilled therapy and adaptive tools without shame, celebrate gains without making them guarantees, and grieve losses that remain. Your usefulness to God is not waiting at the far end of rehabilitation; you belong and can love within today’s actual capacity.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Clarify the medical event, disciplines involved, current function, cognition, communication, swallowing, falls, pain, fatigue, home exercises, equipment, transport, insurance, caregiver capacity, and mood. Reinforce clinician-set precautions, support realistic goals, and prevent helpers from pushing painful or unsafe practice in the name of motivation.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
