    "color": "sky",
    "steps": "Keep the clinician-authored action plan, inhalers or other prescribed treatment, equipment instructions, emergency contacts, and a current medication list accessible. Learn correct device technique from the care team, arrange smoke and irritant reduction, plan rest and accessibility, and identify how the church will support transportation and participation when stamina or infection risk changes. Read Job 33:4: The breath of the Almighty gives me life. Psalm 150:6: Let everything that has breath praise the LORD in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Respiratory Illness + Breathlessness",
    "icon": "🫁",
    "scriptures": "Job 33:4: The breath of the Almighty gives me life. Psalm 150:6: Let everything that has breath praise the LORD. Isaiah 42:5: He who gives breath to the people on it.",
    "definition": "Chronic lung disease, severe asthma, pulmonary fibrosis, and other respiratory conditions can make breathing effortful, limit exertion, disrupt sleep, and create intense fear during flares. Breathlessness is bodily distress, not automatically anxiety, although fear may amplify suffering. Scripture’s language of breath can nourish worship without implying that spiritual effort controls oxygenation or that a person is at fault when lungs require medicine, oxygen, or assisted ventilation. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "respiratoryillness",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Breathlessness can make the next minute feel very small. In those moments, use the medical action plan first and let prayer be simple enough to fit the breath you have. You are not less faithful because worship must be seated, speech is brief, or oxygen equipment is visible. Invite others to adapt pace and environment so that fellowship does not require pretending to have more respiratory capacity than your body currently holds.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Clarify diagnosis, baseline breathing, oxygen or device use, action-plan zones, recent flares, inhaler technique review, infection risk, activity, nutrition, panic, mood, home environment, and caregiver understanding. Never alter oxygen flow, recommend breathing exercises as treatment, or interpret new breathlessness without clinical assessment.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
