    "color": "stone",
    "steps": "Consider the example of the ant to gain wisdom and avoid laziness (Proverbs 6:6). Work heartily in everything you do, as for the Lord rather than for men (Colossians 3:23). Faithfully steward your time and gifts for God to prevent chaos and missed opportunities (Proverbs 6:6). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in its full paragraph, write the desire or fear that most often rules the moment, and name the specific lie it promises. Pray that truth back to God, remove one ordinary provision for the old pattern, practice one concrete opposite act of love, and report the result honestly to a mature believer.",
    "title": "Sloth + Laziness",
    "icon": "🦥",
    "scriptures": "Proverbs 6:6: Go to the ant, O sluggard; consider her ways, and be wise. Colossians 3:23: Work heartily, as for the Lord and not for men.",
    "definition": "Sloth is a resistant avoidance of loving responsibility that may appear as inactivity, distraction, chronic delay, unfinished duty, or refusal of costly effort; bodily limitation and despair must be distinguished from it. Sloth is a state of spiritual apathy and physical idleness characterized by a lack of diligence and a failure to steward God-given responsibilities, often manifesting as a \"defective love\" that prioritizes personal ease over the commands and priorities of God. It is addressed by observing the industriousness of God's creation and committing to work heartily for the Lord rather than for human approval. Scripture locates this struggle within worship and sanctification: people act from the heart, yet embodied weakness, habits, relationships, and circumstances can intensify temptation. The counselee remains an accountable image-bearer who needs both truthful compassion and the Spirit-enabled pattern of putting off sin, renewing the mind, and putting on Christlike love. Change rests on union with Christ and grace, not behavior management or self-salvation.",
    "topicId": "sloth",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Sloth is more than tiredness. It is a settled avoidance of love’s responsibilities, often protected by distraction, delay, excuses, or resentment. Yet not every reduced capacity is laziness; illness, depression, disability, grief, exhaustion, and unclear expectations must be taken seriously. Christ tells the truth about both creaturely weakness and chosen neglect. He gives mercy for confession and strength for the next duty rather than demanding dramatic promises about a whole new life tomorrow.\n\nBegin with the specific responsibility you are avoiding. Make it small enough to start, remove the distraction that repeatedly wins, and ask someone to share accountability. Work is not a way to earn identity; it is one way to love God and neighbor with the strength available today. Repentance may look ordinary: getting up, answering the message, keeping the appointment, finishing a bounded task, or asking for instruction instead of hiding. Receive rest at its proper time so that rest does not become endless escape. If capacity remains unusually low, seek medical and pastoral evaluation rather than applying harsher shame. Diligence grows through repeated faithfulness, not self-contempt. Christ can make you dependable while teaching you that even productive days do not justify you—His grace does.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Assess sleep, health, depression, workload, skills, fear, and habits; define the next concrete duty, shrink initiation barriers, remove distractions, work in accountable intervals, and connect labor to love of neighbor. Do not call disability, illness, grief, unemployment, or exhaustion laziness without careful evidence and appropriate medical or practical assessment. Pastoral method: gather a concrete history of occasions, thoughts, desires, bodily conditions, access, habits, consequences, and relationships before assigning motives. Interpret the primary passages in their literary and canonical settings, connect indicatives of grace to imperatives of obedience, and formulate a specific put-off, mind-renewal, and put-on plan. Use the ordinary means of grace, accountable church relationships, environmental changes, and measurable practice while refusing speculative heart-reading. Review patterns over time, celebrate honest fruit, address concealment directly, and revise the plan when bodily or situational facts were missed. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
