    "color": "orange",
    "steps": "Learn stroke warning signs and call emergency services immediately if they appear, noting the last known well time. During recovery, obtain the therapy schedule, swallowing and fall precautions, medicine plan, communication supports, transportation, home modifications, and caregiver training. Break goals into small functional steps and preserve the survivor’s direct participation in decisions. Read Psalm 46:1: God is our refuge and strength. Isaiah 40:29: He gives power to the faint in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Stroke + Neurological Recovery",
    "icon": "🧠",
    "scriptures": "Psalm 46:1: God is our refuge and strength. Isaiah 40:29: He gives power to the faint. 1 Corinthians 12:22: The parts of the body that seem to be weaker are indispensable.",
    "definition": "Stroke interrupts blood flow to the brain or causes bleeding and can suddenly affect speech, movement, vision, memory, emotion, swallowing, judgment, and independence. Recovery varies, and transient symptoms can still signal urgent danger. Scripture affirms the full dignity of people whose communication or function has changed and calls the body of Christ to patient, accessible care rather than speaking over them or measuring personhood by speed and ability. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "stroke",
    "pastoralGuidance": "A stroke may change how quickly words come, how your body moves, or how familiar tasks are performed, but it has not reduced your worth or erased your place in the church. Grief and frustration can accompany rehabilitation without meaning that you have abandoned hope. Receive repetition, adaptive tools, rest, and skilled therapy as ordinary means of care, and ask others to slow down enough to hear you rather than finishing every sentence for you.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Clarify stroke type if known, present deficits, cognition, communication method, swallowing, falls, medicines, rehabilitation, mood changes, caregiver training, and the survivor’s own goals. Address depression, emotional lability, neglect, unrealistic expectations, and family burnout, and use supported communication rather than assuming inability to speak equals inability to understand.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
