    "color": "teal",
    "steps": "Before the procedure, confirm instructions about medicines, food and drink, transportation, consent, and who will receive the surgeon’s update. Prepare the home for mobility limits, wound care, meals, equipment, follow-up, and emergency contacts. After discharge, use the written plan for medicines, activity, nutrition, incision care, therapy, and warning signs instead of relying on memory. Read Psalm 121:7-8: The LORD will keep your going out and your coming in. 2 Corinthians 12:9: My grace is sufficient for you in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Surgery + Recovery",
    "icon": "🩹",
    "scriptures": "Psalm 121:7-8: The LORD will keep your going out and your coming in. 2 Corinthians 12:9: My grace is sufficient for you. Galatians 6:2: Bear one another’s burdens.",
    "definition": "Surgery joins a planned medical intervention with real bodily vulnerability, anesthesia, pain, dependence, possible complications, and an uncertain recovery curve. Even a successful operation may temporarily change sleep, mobility, appetite, cognition, work, caregiving, and mood. Scripture honors creaturely weakness and shared burdens; it does not require a patient to prove faith through stoicism, disregard postoperative instructions, or return to responsibility before the body is ready. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "surgeryrecovery",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Recovery often advances unevenly. A harder day does not erase progress, and needing help with ordinary tasks does not reduce your dignity. Receive the surgeon’s restrictions as part of wise stewardship, ask for clarification when instructions conflict, and let the church provide specific forms of care that preserve rest rather than create the work of hosting visitors. Your identity in Christ remains steady while productivity, appearance, and independence are temporarily altered.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Gather the procedure, expected course, actual restrictions, pain and sleep pattern, medication access, transportation, home safety, wound-care support, follow-up schedule, and the patient’s understanding of warning signs. Distinguish expected postoperative distress from symptoms the surgical team has said require prompt contact, and screen for depression, confusion, misuse of pain medicine, or caregiver overload.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
