    "steps": "Ask the treating team what is changing, what to expect, which symptoms can be managed, whom to call day or night, and whether palliative care or hospice is appropriate. Review advance directives, surrogate authority, resuscitation preferences, medicines, equipment, funeral wishes, dependents, finances, reconciliation needs, and who may visit or receive updates. Read John 11:35: Jesus wept. Psalm 116:15: Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of his saints in context, asking what faithful dependence and neighbor-love look like in this actual circumstance rather than using the passage as a medical promise. Before the next conversation, write a one-page summary of current facts, the next clinical step, three unanswered questions, one practical need, one person authorized to help, and the exact emergency instructions supplied by the care team. Set a definite follow-up after the next appointment or change in condition, and revise the plan when capacity, diagnosis, or treatment changes.",
    "title": "Terminal Illness + End-of-Life Care",
    "icon": "🕊️",
    "scriptures": "John 11:35: Jesus wept. Psalm 116:15: Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of his saints. Revelation 21:4: He will wipe away every tear.",
    "definition": "Terminal illness brings the likelihood of approaching death into medical decisions, relationships, unfinished responsibilities, symptom care, and spiritual preparation. Hospice and palliative care can support comfort, communication, and family needs without declaring that the person’s remaining life lacks value. Scripture names death as an enemy, permits deep grief, and grounds Christian hope in Christ’s resurrection rather than denial or pressure for a dramatic cure. Medical facts must come from the treating team and reliable health authorities, while pastoral care attends to fear, grief, worship, relationships, conscience, practical burdens, and hope. The guide therefore distinguishes suffering received from sinful responses that may arise within suffering without searching for a hidden offense to explain disease.",
    "topicId": "endoflifecare",
    "pastoralGuidance": "You may speak honestly about death without surrendering hope. There is room to pray for healing, receive comfort-focused care, settle responsibilities, bless loved ones, confess sin, hear the gospel, and rest when strength is small. You do not owe anyone a particular emotional performance. Christ’s hold on you does not weaken when treatment changes, speech fades, or consciousness becomes limited.\n\nIllness can expose how much ordinary life depends on a body that usually works without notice. You may bring disappointment, anger, fear, weariness, and unanswered prayer to God without disguising them. Refuse explanations that blame you for becoming ill or guarantee that enough faith will produce a chosen outcome. At the same time, let suffering reveal places where truth, reconciliation, patience, or help from others is needed, addressing those matters because love is good rather than because they caused the condition.\n\nUse qualified medical care as a gift of common grace. Ask questions, seek a reasonable second opinion when needed, follow the agreed plan, and tell clinicians honestly about side effects, cost, missed treatment, or practices you are considering. Invite the church into specific tasks—transportation, meals, childcare, notes, respite, accessible fellowship, prayer, and steady contact—while preserving your privacy and voice. Christ’s resurrection does not make bodily suffering unreal; it promises that weakness, disability, and death will not have the final word. Until then, faithfulness can be quiet, dependent, and measured in the next truthful step.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Clarify prognosis as communicated by clinicians, capacity, goals, symptoms, hospice or palliative involvement, advance documents, family conflict, unresolved guilt, spiritual fears, dependents, and desired rituals. Coordinate closely with the medical team and avoid declaring the timing of death, demanding continued burdensome treatment, or portraying comfort care as suicide.\n\nPastoral method: begin with the person’s account of bodily experience, clinical facts, losses, relationships, present capacity, and interpretation of God before offering conclusions. Distinguish diagnosis from prognosis, emergency from chronic burden, lament from unbelief, imposed shame from actual guilt, and wise dependence from passivity. Interpret Scripture in its literary and redemptive context; do not turn descriptive healings into promises, assign a specific sin as the cause of illness, or make symptom improvement the test of faith.\n\nExplain confidentiality and its safety limits, obtain consent before contacting clinicians or relatives, and preserve the patient’s decision-making voice. Coordinate practical congregational care with medical social work, rehabilitation, palliative care, licensed mental-health treatment, legal counsel, or protective services when the situation requires expertise beyond pastoral competence. Review the care plan after clinical transitions and watch for caregiver collapse, financial exploitation, medication barriers, suicidality, spiritual abuse, and isolation. Evaluate ministry by truthful engagement with care, growing ability to receive and give love, wise stewardship within actual capacity, and hope rooted in Christ—not by cure, independence, or emotional composure.",
