    "safety": "Time-sensitive emergencies may require rapid consent processes, but coercion, deception, and unauthorized decision making remain serious concerns. A pastor must not sign as surrogate without lawful authority, interpret an advance directive independently, or tell a patient to stop treatment. New incapacity, acute deterioration, or conflict threatening immediate safety requires the medical team and appropriate ethics or legal channels. Pastoral caregivers must not diagnose, prescribe, change medication or equipment, interpret tests, promise healing, or recommend that prayer, fasting, supplements, essential oils, diets, or alternative practices replace qualified care. When danger may be present, stay with the person when safe, contact the appropriate emergency or clinical service, preserve clear access for responders, and continue pastoral presence alongside—not instead of—professional treatment.",
