    "color": "emerald",
    "steps": "Do not love the world or the things in the world, recognizing that love for the world is incompatible with love for the Father (1 John 2:15). Refuse to be conformed to the values and priorities of this world (Romans 12:2). Be transformed by the renewal of your mind to discern and follow God's will (Romans 12:2). End with a specific plan: one truth to study in context, one action of obedient love, one person who will walk alongside, and a definite time for follow-up. Before the next conversation, read every listed passage in its full paragraph, write the desire or fear that most often rules the moment, and name the specific lie it promises. Pray that truth back to God, remove one ordinary provision for the old pattern, practice one concrete opposite act of love, and report the result honestly to a mature believer.",
    "title": "Worldliness",
    "icon": "🌍",
    "scriptures": "1 John 2:15: Do not love the world or the things in the world. If anyone loves the world, the love of the Father is not in him. Romans 12:2: Do not be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewal of your mind.",
    "definition": "Worldliness adopts the fallen age’s loves, boasts, fears, and measures of success in resistance to the Father, even while outward behavior may remain religious. Worldliness is the pursuit of desires and values that prioritize the world over the Father, requiring a transformation of the mind to resist conformity and discern God's will. Scripture locates this struggle within worship and sanctification: people act from the heart, yet embodied weakness, habits, relationships, and circumstances can intensify temptation. The counselee remains an accountable image-bearer who needs both truthful compassion and the Spirit-enabled pattern of putting off sin, renewing the mind, and putting on Christlike love. Change rests on union with Christ and grace, not behavior management or self-salvation.",
    "topicId": "worldliness",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Worldliness is not the same as cultural difference, enjoyment of created things, or failure to prefer another Christian’s taste. It is the pattern of loving the present age’s promises—status, pleasure, autonomy, wealth, visibility, power—more than the Father and His kingdom. The answer is not anxious rule-making or nostalgia. It is a renewed love for Christ that exposes the world’s promises as too small and trains discernment in the actual places where allegiance is contested.\n\nAsk which voices are discipling your imagination and what kind of life they call blessed. Notice where you edit conviction to avoid exclusion or consume without gratitude and self-control. Then change both input and practice: limit what repeatedly shapes disordered desire, deepen worship and fellowship, give generously, choose purity, honor truth, and accept the social cost of obedience. Do not retreat into a Christian subculture that merely baptizes its own pride and consumerism. God sends His people into the world as neighbors and witnesses, not as imitators or contemptuous spectators. You can enjoy art, work, food, technology, and community as gifts while refusing their claim to define the good life. The kingdom of Christ is more solid, beautiful, and enduring than the age now passing away.",
    "counselorGuidance": "The counselor should resist treating the topic as a label and instead learn its history, frequency, severity, triggers, consequences, relationships, bodily factors, and the counselee’s interpretation of God, self, and neighbor. Identify the admired promise and social pressure, compare it with God’s kingdom, confess compromised allegiance, limit formative inputs, deepen worship and Christian fellowship, and practice costly obedience in the contested area. Cultural difference is not automatically worldliness; judgments must arise from Scripture rather than nostalgia, taste, class, ethnicity, or personal preference. Pastoral method: gather a concrete history of occasions, thoughts, desires, bodily conditions, access, habits, consequences, and relationships before assigning motives. Interpret the primary passages in their literary and canonical settings, connect indicatives of grace to imperatives of obedience, and formulate a specific put-off, mind-renewal, and put-on plan. Use the ordinary means of grace, accountable church relationships, environmental changes, and measurable practice while refusing speculative heart-reading. Review patterns over time, celebrate honest fruit, address concealment directly, and revise the plan when bodily or situational facts were missed. The counselor should remain under church accountability, explain confidentiality and its limits, seek consultation when competence is exceeded, and keep the goal centered on knowing Christ, loving God and neighbor, and walking faithfully—not merely reducing discomfort or producing compliance.",
