    "color": "amber",
    "steps": "Bring the full financial record into the light: accounts, cards, payment services, hidden purchases, returns, debt, subscriptions, and money borrowed from others. Record every spending episode for two weeks, including the occasion, desire, promise, purchase, concealment, and consequence. Study Luke 12:15, Proverbs 21:20, Ephesians 4:28, 1 Timothy 6:6-10, and Hebrews 13:5 in context. Confess deception, coveting, presumption, theft, or neglect specifically; make restitution where another person's resources were used. Remove immediate access to credit and shopping triggers, establish a written needs-based budget with accountable review, and practice the opposite pattern through work, contentment, generosity, and faithful payment of obligations. Set a weekly review with a mature believer and a qualified financial counselor when debt or legal obligations require expertise.",
    "title": "Compulsive Spending + Buying",
    "icon": "🛒",
    "scriptures": "Luke 12:15: Take care, and be on your guard against all covetousness. Proverbs 21:20: Precious treasure and oil are in a wise man's dwelling, but a foolish man devours it. Ephesians 4:28: Let the thief no longer steal, but rather let him labor... so that he may have something to share.",
    "definition": "Compulsive spending is a repeated pattern of buying, browsing, borrowing, or financial risk that increasingly rules desire and continues despite foreseeable harm. A purchase may promise comfort, excitement, identity, status, control, escape, or relief from shame; secrecy, rationalization, unused possessions, consumer debt, broken agreements, and neglected provision often follow. Scripture names covetousness as idolatry and treats stewardship, truth, contentment, labor, provision, debt, and generosity as moral matters. This does not mean every severe spending episode has one explanation. Mania or hypomania, cognitive decline, medication effects, exploitation, coercive control, developmental limitations, and genuine poverty require competent assessment. Those factors may shape capacity and care, but they must not become a blanket excuse for deliberate deceit or irresponsible consumption. Sin is serious because it worships created things, presumes upon future provision, harms dependents and creditors, and trains the heart to seek life in acquisition. Grace brings the pattern into light and produces honest, accountable stewardship.",
    "topicId": "compulsive-spending",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Your spending choices matter before God and neighbor. Compulsive spending is not made harmless by calling it retail therapy, collecting, a hobby, self-care, generosity, or a private weakness. Money entrusted by God represents labor, promises, provision, and opportunities to love. When buying consumes what was owed to a spouse, child, creditor, church, employee, or neighbor—and when the pattern is defended with concealment—the sin reaches beyond the receipt. It can involve covetousness, falsehood, presumption, theft, envy, neglect of household duties, and refusal of creaturely limits. God takes those injuries seriously.\n\nCome into the light with the whole record, not a selected total. Christ's mercy is not permission to retain secret accounts; it is power to confess without self-salvation and to accept consequences without despair. Cancel access that repeatedly supplies the pattern. Return what can honestly be returned, sell what should not be kept, stop using future income as though it were guaranteed, and establish a budget that honors existing obligations before discretionary desire. Where others were deceived, confess plainly and make a restitution plan. Learn to endure the urge without obeying it and to name what the purchase promises. Replace acquisition with gratitude, repair, work, giving, hospitality, and enjoyment of what is already present. If elevated mood, little need for sleep, racing thoughts, cognitive change, or inability to control dangerous spending is present, obtain prompt medical or licensed assessment while pastoral accountability continues.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Pastoral method: establish facts, interpret the controlling texts in context, identify specific guilt without speculative heart-reading, and join gospel hope to measurable repentance. Do not counsel from estimates. Gather statements, balances, interest rates, payment history, subscriptions, return patterns, delivery concealment, shared-account access, prior agreements, income, dependents, taxes, and legal obligations. Map representative episodes from trigger through aftermath. Ask about mood elevation, sleep, medication changes, attention or cognitive changes, substance use, gambling, trauma, domestic coercion, and suicidality after financial discovery. Meet separately when a spouse may be financially controlling or abusive; ordinary joint budgeting can expose a victim to retaliation. Distinguish poverty and necessary expense from consumption, generosity from image management, and a regretted purchase from life-dominating practice. Require truthful disclosure to the properly affected people, not indiscriminate public confession. Use temporary spending limits, removal of stored cards, delayed-purchase rules, account alerts, return or sale plans, a realistic budget, debt advice from qualified professionals, and weekly church accountability. Measure repentance by truth, submitted access, kept agreements, restitution, increased provision and generosity, and patient contentment over time—not tears, promises, or a short no-buy streak.",
