    "color": "rose",
    "steps": "List the gifts, labor, patience, access, and mercy you have begun to treat as owed. Identify one recent response to denial, delay, correction, service, or another person's success that exposed entitlement. Study Deuteronomy 8:11-18, Luke 17:7-19, Romans 1:21, James 1:16-17, and 1 Thessalonians 5:18 in context. Confess the specific demand and the people you used, ignored, or punished. Express thanks without qualification, repay or serve where appropriate, accept one ordinary inconvenience without insisting on special treatment, and establish a daily practice of thanking God and people for particular gifts. Review progress weekly with someone who will confront scorekeeping and help you distinguish a rightful request from a ruling demand.",
    "title": "Ingratitude + Entitlement",
    "icon": "🎁",
    "scriptures": "Romans 1:21: Although they knew God, they did not honor him as God or give thanks to him. James 1:17: Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above. Luke 17:17: Were not ten cleansed? Where are the nine?",
    "definition": "Ingratitude refuses to recognize God and neighbors as givers; entitlement converts desires, roles, sacrifices, opportunities, comfort, attention, or mercy into debts others supposedly owe. The pattern forgets dependence, magnifies deprivation, minimizes received good, and responds to limits with resentment, pressure, withdrawal, or contempt. Scripture does not treat thanklessness as poor etiquette. It places refusal to honor and thank God near the root of idolatrous exchange and repeatedly commands grateful worship. Entitlement can exploit family, church, employees, donors, caregivers, or strangers while presenting personal preference as justice. Genuine rights, lawful wages, covenant obligations, restitution, accessibility, and protection from abuse are not entitlement. Counsel must never use gratitude language to silence a person seeking what love or justice truly requires.",
    "topicId": "ingratitude-entitlement",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Entitlement says, often without words, 'My desire creates your obligation.' It interprets ordinary service as the minimum, correction as disrespect, waiting as mistreatment, and another person's freedom as betrayal. Ingratitude then erases the people whose labor supplied what was received and ultimately refuses to honor God as giver. This sin damages marriages, families, churches, workplaces, and mercy ministries because no gift remains a gift and no amount of service settles the account.\n\nThe gospel does not tell you that you deserve nothing so powerful people may exploit you. God names real obligations and condemns withheld wages, broken covenants, partiality, and oppression. But before God, salvation is mercy, every ability is received, and creaturely life is dependent. Confess where you demanded, manipulated, sulked, threatened, or withheld love when preferences were denied. Name the people whose work you ignored and thank them specifically without turning gratitude into performance. Practice receiving ordinary provisions as gifts, honoring limits, waiting without punishment, serving without keeping score, and giving without purchasing control. Christ, to whom all honor belongs, took the form of a servant. Union with Him dismantles both superiority and self-pity and creates grateful, generous love.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Pastoral method: gather concrete episodes, interpret gratitude and service texts in context, distinguish true justice from ruling demand, and connect confession to restitution and practiced service. Ask for concrete examples involving service, money, sex, attention, schedules, leadership, hospitality, family roles, disability accommodations, ministry recognition, and response to 'no.' Separate commanded obligation, wise expectation, explicit agreement, and personal preference. Investigate whether the person has been denied justice or conditioned by abuse to call basic safety selfish; do not reinforce exploitation. Where entitlement is present, identify the internal rule, the punishment used when others do not comply, and the people made responsible for emotional stability. Assign specific thanks, correction of exaggerations, restitution for exploited labor or resources, service in low-visibility tasks, and practice accepting lawful limits. Explore family and cultural patterns without turning explanation into excuse. Measure change by reduced scorekeeping, truthful requests, respect for consent and limits, prompt gratitude, generosity, and steady service when no praise follows. Guard against coerced gratitude toward an offender or institution that has not told the truth.",
