    "steps": "Identify where wealth, appearance, ethnicity, education, politics, family, disability, usefulness, influence, or agreement changes the honor, patience, access, protection, or discipline you offer. Study Leviticus 19:15, James 2:1-13, 1 Timothy 5:21, and 1 Corinthians 12:21-26 in context. Confess concrete unequal treatment rather than only claiming impartial intentions. Correct the record, restore access or opportunity where lawful, repair harmed relationships without demanding immediate trust, and invite review from people able to see the pattern. Practice equal truth, patient listening, and costly honor toward someone who cannot advance your status.",
    "title": "Partiality + Favoritism",
    "icon": "⚖️",
    "scriptures": "James 2:1: Show no partiality as you hold the faith in our Lord Jesus Christ, the Lord of glory. Leviticus 19:15: You shall do no injustice in court. You shall not be partial to the poor or defer to the great. 1 Timothy 5:21: Do nothing from partiality.",
    "definition": "Partiality assigns honor, credibility, access, patience, opportunity, protection, or judgment according to status and self-interest rather than truth and neighbor-love. It may favor the wealthy or poor, attractive or influential, familiar family, preferred race or culture, political ally, donor, leader, high performer, able-bodied person, or anyone whose approval seems useful. Scripture calls such distinction evil reasoning, injustice, and contradiction of faith in the Lord of glory. Favoritism can be personal or embedded in repeated policies and informal networks. Not every distinction is partiality: responsibilities, qualifications, need, repentance, safety, and lawful authority may require different treatment. Serious care examines the morally relevant reason for a decision and its actual fruit rather than hiding behind claimed neutrality.",
    "topicId": "partiality",
    "pastoralGuidance": "Partiality often wears respectable clothing: chemistry, loyalty, standards, discretion, donor care, family preference, cultural fit, or protecting the ministry. James tears away that covering. To honor the impressive while making the vulnerable stand at the edge is to judge with evil thoughts and deny the character of Christ's kingdom. The sin can determine whose story is believed, whose misconduct is excused, who receives development, whom leaders befriend, which child is celebrated, and whose pain is considered inconvenient. Its consequences are not abstract; people are exposed, silenced, passed over, disciplined unequally, or taught that worldly status governs the household of God.\n\nRepentance requires more than announcing that everyone is welcome. Ask who receives your unhurried attention and who receives suspicion; whose failure you interpret generously; who has access to decisions; and whose usefulness purchases protection. Confess actual choices to those properly affected. Correct unequal standards, reopen fair processes, make restitution where possible, and accept that harmed people may not trust a sudden promise. Christ did not save according to social value. He creates one body whose apparently weaker members receive indispensable honor. Learn to offer truth without flattery, correction without scapegoating, and hospitality without calculating return.",
    "counselorGuidance": "Pastoral method: compare concrete decisions, interpret biblical impartiality in context, distinguish relevant differences from worldly status, and require repair proportionate to harm. Review decisions, not only attitudes: invitations, referrals, discipline, compensation, scheduling, platform access, child treatment, leadership response, credibility judgments, accommodations, and confidentiality. Compare similar cases and document why different treatment occurred. Ask who benefits, who bears risk, and whether the stated criterion is biblical, lawful, consistently applied, and actually relevant. Hear affected people without forcing them into direct confrontation or making them prove hidden motives. Distinguish partiality from prudent safeguarding, qualification, triage by need, or recognizing demonstrated character. Where leaders or institutions are involved, use independent review and conflict-of-interest safeguards; do not let the favored group investigate itself. Repentance may require public correction proportionate to public harm, restored opportunity, financial or practical restitution, policy revision, discipline, and long observation. Avoid tokenism and compelled intimacy. Measure fruit by consistent standards, transparent processes, protection of vulnerable people, shared honor, and willingness to bear cost when favoritism previously brought advantage.",
