    "safety": "Church hurt may include criminal abuse, child or vulnerable-adult maltreatment, sexual assault, domestic violence, stalking, threats, self-harm, suicidal intent, medical crisis, financial exploitation, or retaliation by people with spiritual and social power. Meet privately, explain confidentiality and reporting limits, ask behavior-specific questions, determine whether devices or communications are monitored, and follow current jurisdiction-specific reporting law. Imminent danger requires emergency help; serious injury, strangulation, sexual assault, poisoning, or head injury requires prompt medical attention. Do not alert an alleged offender, arrange a surprise confrontation, disclose the reporter’s location or safety plan, or store sensitive material where discovery could increase risk.\n\nPastoral care must not be conditioned on silence, nondisclosure agreements, withdrawal of a police report, continued attendance, marital reunion, private confrontation, or acceptance of an internal finding. Never send an allegation back to the accused leader for sole handling. Use qualified external investigators when institutional conduct or senior leadership is implicated, and preserve civil evidence according to lawful professional guidance. A person who leaves an unsafe congregation is not thereby leaving Christ. At the same time, counselors should assess false or malicious accusation, memory uncertainty, mutual sinful conflict, and disproportionate conclusions with the same commitment to impartial truth. Safety protects the accused from rumor as well as the vulnerable from power, but uncertainty must never be used as permission for preventable exposure.",
